# DESCRIPTION
# Using watching times of streaming shows, perform the initial steps of hypothesis test
# and interpret the results.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('WeBWorK')
## DBchapter('Nursing-Med Calc')
## DBsection('Problem')
## KEYWORDS('null hypothesis', 'alternative hypothesis', 'two-sample test')
## Author('Catherine Buell')
## Institution('Fitchburg State')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",   'PGML.pl',
    'randomPerson.pl', "parserPopUp.pl",
    "PGcourse.pl"
);

Context("Numeric");
Context()->flags->set(
    tolerance => 0.02,
    tolType   => "absolute",
);

@opts = ('\(<\)', '\(>\)', '\(=\)', '\(\neq\)');
$dd1  = DropDown([@opts], 2, useHTMLSelect => 0);
$dd2  = DropDown([@opts], 3, useHTMLSelect => 0);

do {
    $n1 = random(50, 85, 5);
    $n2 = random(40, 60);
} until $n1 != $n2;

$mean_diff = random(4, 8, 0.1);

$p = randomPerson();

$sig_level = list_random(1, 5, 10, 20);
$pval      = random(1, 9) * list_random(0.001, 0.0001, 0.00001);

$dd3 = DropDown(
    [
        "We will reject the null hypothesis.",
        "We fail to reject the null hypothesis."
    ],
    0
);

BEGIN_PGML

Streaming services keep track of the total minutes that people view each episode
of shows. One particular streaming service published a sample of their recent
data. A curious competition show fan wants to know whether the mean number
of minutes viewed is different for cooking competitions (group 1) vs craft
competitions (group 2). There are [$n1] episodes of cooking competitions
in the sample and [$n2] episodes of craft competitions. She finds a mean 
difference of cooking competitions being watched for [$mean_diff] fewer 
minutes. She does a hypothesis test at a [$sig_level]% level of significance.

a) The null hypothesis would be: [`H_0`]: [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd1} [`\mu_{2}`]

b) The alternative hypothesis would be: [`H_a`]:  [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd2}   [`\mu_{2}`]

c) What is [`n_1`]? [____________]{$n1}

d) What is [`n_2`]? [____________]{$n2}

e) What is [`\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2`] (to two decimal places)? [____________]{$mean_diff }

f) If the p-value is [$pval], what should be our conclusion? [_]{$dd3}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) The null hypothesis is the neutral relationship and that is [`\mu_1=\mu_2`].

b) Since the fan is interested if there is a difference in the mean number of minutes viewed, then the alternative hypothesis is [` \mu_1 \neq \mu_2 `]. 

c) [`n_1 = [$n1]`]

d) [`n_1 = [$n2]`]

e) The mean difference is that found difference in the problem so 
    [`\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2 = [$mean_diff].`]

f) Since the [`p`]-value is smaller than the level of significant of [`\alpha = [$sig_level/100]`], converted to a fraction, she should *reject the null hypothesis*.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
